# No Individual Patches (Awesome PG)

Never individually repair, hardcode, special-case, or manually alter a resident's data to make a bug appear fixed.

Individual resident records may **only** be used to: reproduce the bug, inspect state, understand the failure, verify the generic fix afterward.

## Scope

All Awesome PG modules: residents, bookings, rooms/beds, room changes, move-outs, deposits, refunds, rent, electricity, invoices, payments, wallets, billing, notifications, admin operations, resident portal, staff/workforce, pricing, settlements.

## Required workflow

1. Diagnose using the affected resident.
2. Find the underlying **generic** cause in shared code/workflow.
3. Fix the shared application/service/workflow/database logic.
4. Add a regression test.
5. Verify against **multiple** resident states (normal booking, room change, no due, pending approval, etc.).
6. If historical data reconciliation is needed, use a **generic, auditable, idempotent** mechanism for **all** affected records — ask before any data repair.

## Never

- Hardcode resident/booking/invoice IDs or amounts for one case
- One-off SQL or manual production financial changes to hide a software bug
- Fake/default financial values or blanket exception suppression without root cause

## Distinguish clearly

- **A. Fix the software** (always first)
- **B. Reconcile historical data** (only after generic fix; never one-resident patches)

## Bug report must state

Root cause · shared code/workflow · generic fix · regression test · affected population · whether historical reconciliation is needed · how reconciliation works generically · deployment status

**Goal:** Make Awesome PG work correctly for **every** resident — not make one resident work.
